-- title:
--   Artin reciprocity at a modulus admissible for exponent n
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be number fields with $L$ an extension of $K$ that is Galois, whose Galois group $L \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[K]} L$ is commutative. Let $n$ be a nonzero natural number such that $x^{n} = 1$ for every $x$ in the Galois group, and let $\mathfrak f$ be an ideal of $\mathcal O_K$ which is admissible at degree $n$ for $L/K$, meaning: $\mathfrak f \neq \bot$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_K$ whose chosen prime `primeAbove K L v` of $\mathcal O_L$ has nontrivial inertia subgroup in the Galois group, the power $v^{e(v)}$ divides $\mathfrak f$, where $e(v) = 1 + \sum_{p \mid n} (\mathrm{ord}_p(n) + 1)\, e'(p\mathbb Z, v)$, the sum running over the prime factors of $n$ and $e'$ denoting the ramification index of $v$ over the ideal generated by $p$. Then the Artin symbol `artinSymbol K L 𝔣`, the homomorphism from the group of units of fractional ideals $I$ of $K$ with $\mathrm{count}_v(I) = 0$ for all primes $v$ dividing $\mathfrak f$ to the Galois group, obtained from the assignment sending each prime $v$ to the arithmetic Frobenius at `primeAbove K L v`, is surjective, and its kernel equals the norm-ray subgroup: the join of the narrow ray subgroup modulo $\mathfrak f$, intersected with the group of ideals coprime to $\mathfrak f$, with the image of the relative norm `relNormCTM K L 𝔣` of the fractional ideals of $L$ coprime to the extended modulus.
--
--   This is Artin's reciprocity law in ray-class form for an abelian extension of number fields, in the shape needed later: the modulus is a single nonzero ideal, divisible at each ramified prime by an explicit power depending on $n$ and on ramification over the prime factors of $n$, and the ray is the narrow one. It generalises the prime-power case [`LanglandsTunnell.Artin.artinSymbol_surjective_and_ker_eq_normRaySubgroup_of_isAdmissibleModulusOfDegree`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.Artin.artinSymbol_surjective_and_ker_eq_normRaySubgroup_of_isAdmissibleModulusOfDegree) to arbitrary exponents $n$, and is used to produce ray-class characters with prescribed local behaviour in the Langlands–Tunnell input to the argument.
